WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.590 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  medicine  is miraculous we have developed drugs  that can go into individual cells change  the dna  and cure genetic diseases  we have developed robots that can do  surgery  through an incision no bigger than a  coin  we were even able to make a vaccine  against covid19  in under 12 months  but let me tell you how these miracles  play out  on the ground chris was a 63 year old  man  he grew up in a family of five and he  took his first job  selling carpets door to door eventually  he worked his way up and opened a carpet  store  in the evenings he enjoyed a glass of  wine with dinner  he'd smoked since he was a teenager and  he had high blood pressure  chris hated going to see the doctor  he could never get through on the phone  he could never find a parking space  and he never saw the same doctor twice  but one year after getting back from  holiday  he had some stomach pain that just  wasn't going away  so he decided to make an appointment  when he walked into the doctor's office  that friday morning  he was greeted with a familiar sight and  sounds of a full waiting room  and you can see why the doctor only had  15 minutes to see each patient  and in that time she had to go over  their previous records  find them from the waiting room talk to  them perform an examination form a  diagnosis  prescribe treatments make any referrals  explain it to the patient  and finally write everything down  but chris's case was routine  he just got back from holiday and  stomach pain is really common  so the doctor just said take these  medicines and come back in a few weeks  if it's no better  but a few weeks passed and the pain  hadn't gone away  so chris made another appointment indeed  this time  he saw a different doctor and they came  to the same conclusion  saying it's probably the same thing  take the medicine for a bit longer we'll  send some blood tests just to be sure  and we'll get in touch if anything's  wrong  but a few weeks passed and chris was  feeling  just awful so he went back  for a third time taking yet another day  off work  and when he got there it was clear that  something was very wrong  he'd developed a life-threatening  infection  and required urgent admission to  hospital when he got to the hospital  it turned out the infection was being  caused by a cancer  they managed to clear the infection but  the cancer was inoperable  and six months later chris passed away  what is so extraordinary  about his story is just how ordinary it  is  we have all of these amazing  technologies  but we can't get the basics right in  reality  i saw hundreds of patients like chris  whilst working as a doctor  he'd actually had scans at another  hospital the year before  but they weren't available to the doctor  when she was making diagnosis  he had blood tests sent but they weren't  checked and followed up in a timely  manner  because the doctor had no time allocated  to do so and no software or technology  to help her flag abnormal results  and finally guidelines suggest that  patients like chris  should be referred for immediate  investigations after their first visit  but with the amount and volume of  information that's being published  every day there's no chance any human  could possibly stay on top of it  without the help of technology  i tell you chris's story  not because he was one of my patients  but because he was my father  and it was going on his patient journey  with him that made me realize just how  big the gulf is  between what is possible and that  which everyday people actually receive  in healthcare  inefficiencies error and waste in  healthcare  cost one trillion dollars  every year and they leave the  preventable death  of 250 000 americans to put that in  context  that is the same amount of money as the  entire economy of mexico  healthcare is broken but i believe  that with three principles we can fix it  design for patients equip clinicians for  success  and align industry incentives  design for patients when it comes to  healthcare  patients are forced to navigate an  impossibly complex and opaque  landscape of insurers payers  providers and when they do receive care  it's in a way that is most convenient  for the clinician  not the patient  this wouldn't happen in any other  industry  you can order take away a taxi  or even access heavily regulated  industries like banking from tap  the phone why should healthcare be any  different  patients deserve better  you should demand healthcare that is  high quality  easy to navigate and delivered in a way  that is convenient to you  and you can vote with your feet get  health insurance policies that support  providers that are delivering just this  sort of service  equip clinicians for success  when you think of the cutting edge of  industry what do you think of  perhaps companies that use ai machine  learning  share information using platforms like  slack  or at the very least maybe they use a  computer rather than paper  how would they do without any of these  when i worked for the uk's national  health service i worked  a stone's throw away from the uk's  epicenter for digital health  affectionately known as silicon  roundabout  yet i sent referrals by fax  contacted colleagues by pager and wrote  prescriptions  on paper and this is the case all over  the world  it takes on average 17 years for  innovations to move from concept  to clinic when it comes to clinicians  being able to deliver excellent care we  cannot change the human condition  but we can change the conditions in  which humans work  patients deserve better providers need  to equip doctors with the tools to  deliver care  in an efficient and convenient manner  they need to celebrate the operational  the mundane the bread and butter not  just the headline making discoveries  it is much better to have a simple  machine that functions well  than a complex machine that barely  functions at all  align industry incentives  the author of the armchair economist  stephen linzberg  described all of economics in four words  people respond to incentives  and healthcare is constructed in a way  the volume  not outcomes are incentivized fix as  many hips as possible perform as many  scans as possible  prescribe as many drugs as possible  and these incentives are key drivers of  provider behavior  but not all action is good unnecessary  surgery can lead to debilitating side  effects  unnecessary scans expose patients to  radiation  that increases the risk of cancer and  the overuse of prescription medications  can lead to dependency and addiction  patients deserve better  policymakers need to construct  incentives that reward  health not wealth  and as consumers of healthcare you need  to ask is this treatment in my best  interest  i believe that it is in the operational  but overlooked  that there is the biggest opportunity to  improve healthcare  i believe that working together we can  create a healthcare system  with people like my father in mind one  that puts patients first  equips clinicians for success and  incentivizes health  not wealth so i invite you to join me  and help put the miracle back in  medicine  thank you

